## 1. Introduction
Recent advances in biomedicine and chemistry have provided unique opportunities to design novel biomaterials that rationally act on abnormal molecules and biochemical signals leading to various diseases [1,2]. Although great efforts have been devoted for the development of novel drugs in the past few decades, the efficacy of chemotherapy remains limited [3–5]. A major obstacle for successful chemotherapy with maximum therapeutic effect and minimum side-effect lies in the intrinsic properties of drugs, such as poor solubility, insufficient in vitro and in vivo stability, and low bioavailability [6]. In addition, risk of toxicity to normal tissue and cells can be increased by the direct delivery of drug through transport within a vessel, e.g. blood circulation [7–9].
To tackle the above limitations, controlled drug release system with appropriate transport vehicles (carriers) has recently been proposed [9,10]. Once the drug carrier has concentrated at the diseased tissue and cells, the drug can be released either by changing physiological conditions, such as pH, and temperature or enzymatic activity. Recently, different pharmaceutical carriers, including soluble polymers, dendrimers, microcapsules, microparticles, liposomes and micelles, have been employed to increase the therapeutically effective concentration by multiplying the number of drug molecules per single event, as well as minimize the sideeffects by controlled release [11–14]. Nevertheless, the search for efficient and safe transport vehicles to improve drug availability at the diseased site has been a challenge yet exciting area of research [14].
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as coordination polymers or coordination networks, are the unique of crystalline materials formed by connecting metal centers and organic ligands into infinite arrays through dative bonds [15–18]. As one types of porous materials, MOFs exhibited potential advantages over conventional materials, such as tunable pore size and shape, adjustable composition and structure, biodegradability, excellent loading capacity and controllable drug release, and versatile functionalities, which enable MOFs to perform as promising platforms for various medical applications, including drug delivery [19–26], molecular sensing and bioimaging [19,27,28], and theranostics [29–32]. Of the reported MOFs, HKUST-1 $(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ , BTC, 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylate) offers an excellent template for the preparation of porous materials [33–37]. Doping with graphene oxide (GO), we also demonstrated its extraordinary capability on the application in wastewater treatment, and drug delivery vehicles [38,39].
Compare to the aforementioned single ligand MOFs, recently reported mixed ligand MOFs showed significant improvement in rational design and construction, which rendered them better performance in heterogeneous catalysis, luminescence sensing, gas storage [40–44]. However, few studies have been conducted for the investigation of mixed ligand MOFs as the transport vehicles for drugs, such as ibuprofen (IBU) and doxorubicin hydrochloride (DOX) [40]. In this work, mixed ligand MOFs, MOFs-2, MOFs-3 were designed and synthesized by rational variation the ratio of two organic ligands, BTC and isophthalic acid (IPA). As the control groups, MOFs-1, and MOF-4 were synthesized with a single ligand of BTC, and IPA, respectively. The results of XRD, FTIR and SEM confirmed the interpenetrated structures of MOFs-2, MOFs-3 (Scheme 1). As expected, mixed ligand MOFs displayed excellent capability in drug delivery vehicles over to single ligand MOFs, MOFs-1, and MOFs-4. In addition, it was found that the drug loading and release performance was correlated with the content of BTC and IPA.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and physical methods
$\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , IPA, $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China), IBU, and DOX were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. MTT (3-(4,5-Dimethylthiazol-2-y l)-2,5-Diphenyltetrazolium Bromide), fetal bovine serum (FBS), Trypsin-EDTA Solution, Eagle’s Minimum Essential Medium (EMEM), L-glutamine, penicillin, and streptomycin sulphate were from Life Technologies, China. Reagents and solvents were of A. R. grade and used without further purification unless otherwise noted. Fresh double distilled water was used throughout the experiment.
The power X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed using a D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer (Bruker Company, USA). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was carried out using a Single frequency infrared spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer, USA). And scanning electron microscope images were obtained using a JSM6510LV scanning electron microscope (JEOL, Japan).
## 2.2. Synthesis of MOFs
Synthesis of MOFs-2. To a solution of $0.2121\mathrm{~g~H_{3}B T C}$ $\cdot1\mathrm{mmol}\rangle$ , and $0.2515{\mathrm{g}}$ IPA $(1.5\mathrm{mmol})$ in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol, $1.0898\mathrm{~g~Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}$ ( $4.5\:\mathrm{mmol}\}$ in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ water was added slowly. After stirring at R. T. for $30\mathrm{min}$ , the mixture was then heated to $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ in a Teflon reaction vessel. The formed precipitate was collected by filtration, and then washed with water and ethanol for three times. The product, MOFs-2, was obtained and dried at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ .
Similar procedure was conducted for the synthesis of MOFs-1, MOFs-3, MOFs-4 by changes of the amount of $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C},$ , and IPA. MOFs-1 $100\%$ IPA), MOFs-2 $40\%$ $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , $60\%$ IPA), MOFs-3 ( $70\%$ $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , $30\%$ IPA), MOFs-4 $100\%\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , HKUST-1).
## 2.3. Cell culture
The normal human cell line, HEP 293A were maintained in EMEM supplemented with $10\%(\mathrm{v/v)}$ fetal bovine serum (FBS), $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{L}}.$ -glutamine $(2{\mathrm{~mM}})$ , sodium pyruvate $(1\mathrm{mM})$ , penicillin $(100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ , streptomycin sulphate $\mathbf{\chi}_{100}\mathbf{\mu}\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L})$ . The cells were grown in a humidified $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\ 5\%\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}/95\%$ air $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}/\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ incubator. The growth medium was changed every two days. The cells were routinely subcultured using $0.05\%$ trypsin-EDTA solution and growth to $80\%$ confluence prior to experiment.
## 2.4. MTT cell viability assays
The MTT assay involves the reduction of a yellow tetrazolium salt, [3-(4,5-dimethylthazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide] tetrazolium to an insoluble formazan crystal by the metabolic activity of living cells [45]. In this work, MTT assay was utilized to investigate the cytotoxicity of MOFs. HEP 293A cells were seeded at a density of $5\times10^{4}\mathrm{cells/mL}$ in a 96-well microassay culture plate and growth $24\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ incubator. MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, MOFs-4, in fresh culture medium were added into each well with different concentrations from 10 to $60~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Culture medium only was employed as the control group, and wells containing culture media without cells were used as blanks. After incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ incubator for $24\mathrm{h},$ cell culture medium was removed and cells were washed three times with PBS. Then, $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ MTT solution in PBS was added to each well, and the cells were incubated for another $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The excess MTT solution was then carefully removed from each well, and the formed formazan was dissolved in $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DMSO (dimethyl sulfoxide). The optical density of each well was then measured at a wavelength of $570\mathrm{nm}$ referenced at $630\mathrm{nm}$ using a microplate reader (Bio-Rad, xMark). The results from the five individual experiments were averaged. The following formula was used to calculate the viability of cell growth: Vialibity $\left(\%\right)=$ (mean of absorbance value of treatment group–blank)/(mean absorbance value of control–blank) $\times100$ .
## 2.5. Drug loading
$0.2{\mathrm{~g~}}$ MOFs were added into a culture bottle with $20\mathrm{mL}\left(1\mathrm{g/L}\right)$ IBU water solution. After being mixed at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ , $48\mathrm{h}$ and $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The drug deposited MOFs were then collected by centrifugation and washed with water for three time. The concentrations of IBU were determined by UV–vis analysis $(\lambda_{a b s,m a x}=260)$ based on the calibration curve of IBU. Similar experimental condition were conducted to load the DOX with MOFs, and the concentrations were measured by UV–vis analysis $(\lambda_{a b s,m a x}=460\mathrm{nm})$ based on the calibration curve of DOX.
## 2.6. Release experiment
The drug release experiments of DOX were performed in a bottle at $37.0^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in pH 3.8, $\mathsf{p H}5.8$ and pH 7.4 PBS buffer solution. The release experiments of IBU were conducted in PBS buffer, $\mathsf{p H7.4}$ Typically, MOFs $\langle100\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ containing a known amount of DOX (IBU) were suspended in a buffer solution. The mixture solution was mechanically stirred and the temperature was maintained at $37.0^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in a water bath. The drug release medium $(3~\mathrm{mL})$ was withdrawn and analysed by UV–vis absorption spectroscopy at a wavelength of $460\mathrm{nm}$ (DOX), $264\mathrm{nm}$ (IBU) at given time intervals and replaced with the same volume of fresh buffer solution.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Design, preparation and characterization of MOFs
Considering the excellent performance of mixed ligand MOFs in practical applications, in this work, we developed a series of Cu complexes-based MOFs with different ligands as the transport vehicles for anti-tumor drug, DOX and anti-inflammation drug, IBU. Four MOFs, MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, MOFs-4, were designed by variation the content of ligand BTC from $0\%$ to $100\%$ , and ligand IPA from $100\%$ to $0\%$ . All MOFs in the present study were synthesized by hydrothermal method with slight modification. Specifically, heating the solution of $\mathtt{E t O H-H_{2}O}$ containing ligands, IPA, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , and meal ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the precipitate was collected and washed as the products of MOFs. The synthesized MOFs were then characterized by PXRD, FTIR, and SEM analysis.
The crystallinity of the prepared MOFs was initially examined by the PXRD (powder X-ray diffraction) method (Fig. 1a). It was noted that the XRD characteristic diffraction peaks of MOFs-4 with $100\%\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ ligand well matched to the reported HKUST-1. MOFs-1, and mixed ligand MOFs-2, MOFs-3 also showed their characteristic diffraction peaks with an obvious difference. All of the diffraction peaks indicated that all of the prepared MOFs were readily indexed to crystalline.
The structure of the synthesized MOFs was then confirmed by FTIR spectra analysis, and the results presented in Fig. 1b. Similar FTIR spectra were observed for all of the synthesized MOFs due to the phenyl monocarboxylic acid groups. For the IR spectra of MOFs-1, the bands centered at $886~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $820~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the $-C\mathsf{H}$ bending vibration of substituted benzene. The stretching vibration band of carboxylate groups in IPA was found at $1610\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . For the infrared spectrum of MOFs-4, the characteristic peaks at $760\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the $-\mathrm{CH}$ vibration of substituted benzene. The asymmetric stretching of the carboxylate groups in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ was observed at $1630{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ , and the symmetric stretching of the carboxylate groups in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ was recorded at $1373\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . An obvious hypochromatic shift of the characteristic absorption peaks of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ $(1720\mathrm{cm}^{-1}),$ was observed, indicating the successful coordination of carboxyl of $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ . Characteristic peak of carbonyl can be observed only at $730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the effective combination of mixed ligands and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ . All of the results demonstrated that the successful coordination of mixed ligands with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ .
The morphology, surface texture of the prepared MOFs were then measured by scanning electron microscope (SEM) imaging. As shown in Fig. 2, MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, MOFs-4 displayed obvious difference in morphology. In contrast to the single ligand based MOFs-1, and MOFs-4, interpenetration structures were obtained for MOFs-2, and MOFs-3, indicating the successful coordination of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ with both mixed ligands (BTC and IPA). As shown in Fig. 2b, combination structure of triangles and neglect the spherical structure in the center was recorded for MOFs-2. The structure of MOFs-3 was found to be similar to MOFs-2.
## 3.2. Surface analysis
The porosity were also confirmed by measuring the nitrogen gas adsorption-desorption isotherm, and the results were shown in Figs. S1–S4. Due to the existence of BTC ligand, similar shape of isotherm was observed for MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4 with certain adsorption capacity in low pressure and a hysteresis loop in the medium and high pressure. Therefore, microporous and mesoporous structure was presented for MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4 due to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I/IV}}$ mixed type isotherm displayed (Figs. S2–S4). In contrast, as shown in Fig. S1, MOFs-1 exhibited III type isotherm. A weak interaction of MOFs-1 with ${\sf N}_{2}$ was observed as the consequence of the macroporous structure of MOFs-1.
The corresponding textural parameters calculated by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were then listed in Table 1. As hypothesized, the porous size of macroporous structure of MOFs-1 was found to be $14.67\:\mathrm{nm}$ , which is around 7-fold larger than MOFs-2 $(2.12\mathrm{nm})$ ), MOFs-3 $(2.09\mathrm{nm})$ , and MOFs-4 $(2.28\mathrm{nm})$ . In addition, BET surface area measurement gave MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4 were 51.79, 308.97, 589.61, and $680.59\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively.
## 3.3. Cytotoxicity
The cytotoxicity of the prepared MOFs was then examined by the reduction activity of the methyl thiazolyl tetrazolium (MTT, [3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide]) assay [46]. Fig. 3 represented the results of HEK 293A cells incubated with different concentration of MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4 (0, 10, 30, $60\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . No significant change in the proliferation of HEK 293A cells was observed after incubation with mixed ligand MOFs for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ incubator. Even after incubation with $60~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ mixed ligand MOFs for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the cellular viability was still greater than $82\%$ , suggesting that the mixed ligand MOFs-2 and MOFs-3, and single ligand MOFs, MOFs-1, MOFs-4 are low cytotoxicity, which enable them to be used as the delivery vehicle for drugs.
## 3.4. Transport vehicles for IBU and DOX
To demonstrate the potential medical application of the prepared mixed ligand MOFs, the performance of loading and release of drugs, IBU and DOX was then examined. IBU is the drug widely used for the management of pain, fever and osteoarthritis. However, widely application of this drug is limited due to the potential side effects which induced by its low solubility, short biological half-life $(2\mathsf{h})$ and rapid clearance after oral administration, high and multiple dosing applied. In the present work, we anticipate that the functional MOFs can be employed as the transport vehicle for IBU.
As shown in Fig. 4a, single ligand MOFs-1 and MOFs-4 exhibited weak capacity on loading of IBU even present in the solution of IBU for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . However, the amount of IBU in mixed ligand MOFs-2, MOFs-3 increased remarkably with the extension of incubation times. In particularly, mixed ligand MOFs-2 ( $40\%$ BTC, and $60\%$ IPA) showed the best performance on IBU loading at $24\mathrm{h}$ and slight increase in loading amount was observed when the incubation time was extended to $48\mathrm{h}$ and $^{72\mathrm{{h}}}$ . It has been reported that the drug loading of MOFs is governed by its porosity of MOFs and the chemical properties of drugs [31]. Our previous research also found that the drug loading capability is size (pore size of MOFs and molecular size of drugs) dependent [47]. In this work, we reasoned that the loading capability is dominated by both the pore size and the interaction of carboxyl group of IBU and MOFs. Although the pore size of MOFs-4 $(2.28\mathrm{nm})$ is similar to MOFs-2 $(2.12\mathrm{nm})$ , and MOFs-3 $(2.09\mathrm{nm})$ , the lower IBU loading could be ascribed by the weak interaction of carboxyl group on IBU with MOFs-4, which was coordinated with $100\%$ BTC. For MOFs-1, the low drug loading capability could be attributed to the large pore size $\cdot14.67\mathrm{nm})$ , where the IBU could be easily released to water (Fig. 4b). Nevertheless, the IBU loading amount of MOFs-1 is around two times higher than MOFs-4, indicating that the interaction of carboxyl group of IBU and MOFs is an important factor for the loading of IBU. Therefore, it is reasonable for that MOFs-2 and MOFs-3 showed better IBU loading capability. In particularly, together with the suitable pore size and the coordination ligands, MOFs-2 exhibited best performance on IBU loading.
To ensure that the structure of MOFs was not effected by the drug loading, PXRD and surface analysis of IBU@MOFs-2 was then conducted. As shown in Fig. 5, it was found that IBU@MOFs-2 remains highly crystalline and of the same structure as the MOFs-2. More important, the result of surface analysis confirmed that both surface area and pore volume were significantly decreased after loading of IBU, and the pore size was also decreased to $1.9\mathrm{nm}$ , suggesting the successful loading of IBU to MOFs-2 (Table 1, Fig. S5). The successful drug loading was further confirmed by the UV–vis analysis and FTIR analysis. As shown in Fig. S6, decrease in absorption spectra of IBU supernatant was also found after the drug loading. As shown in Fig. S7, FTIR absorption of IBU@MOFs-2 was found to be similar to MOFs-2. Only the band at $1042\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was noticed as the skeletal vibration of ACOO stretching. This is not surprise for that the functional groups of IBU are similar to IBU and BTC ligands.
The performance of IBU release of the IBU@MOFs were then examined in PBS buffer and the results were shown in Fig. 4b. It was found that IBU was completely released in MOFs-1, MOFs-2, while partly release of IBU was observed with MOFs-3 $(90\%)$ , and MOFs-4 $(70\%)$ . This could be attributed to the strong capability in absorption of the MOFs with BTC ligand. More important, different with the IBU release in $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ of MOFs-1, MOFs-2 showed moderate release of IBU. Therefore, the results demonstrated that the performance of IBU sustained release by functional MOFs can be mediated by the coordinative BTC ligand, and MOFs-2 with mixed BTC and IPA ligands showed the best performance in IBU loading and release.
DOX is an anthracycline antibiotic widely used for cancer chemotherapy. The major obstacles for the widely application in clinic of DOX lie in its dose-related side effects, such as bone marrow toxicity, gastrointestinal disorders, acute or cumulative cardiotoxicity, and extravasation. In order to minimize the side effects and to improve the therapeutic efficiency of DOX, controlled drug release techniques based on functional materials are in demand. In this work, the performance of functional MOFs as the delivery vehicle for DOX was then evaluated.
As shown in Fig. 6a, different with the control group (MOFs-1), MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4 exhibited significant improvement in DOX loading capability upon the extension of incubation time. We noticed that mixed ligand MOFs-2 showed the best performance in transport DOX as the consequence of highest loading efficiency even at $48\mathrm{h}$ . The results suggested that the loading of DOX is pore size dependent. It is not surprise that MOFs-2, MOFs-3 and MOFs-4 with the suitable pore size could be employed as the porous materials for the delivery of DOX. Furthermore, DOX@MOFs-2 was also highly crystalline and of the same structure as the MOFs2, indicating the structure of MOFs-2 was not effected by loading of DOX (Fig. 5). While the remarkable decreasing of surface area, pore volume and pore size was observed due to the loading of DOX within in MOFs (Table 1, Fig. S8). The successful DOX loading was also investigated by UV–vis and FTIR analysis, and the results were illustrated in Figs. S9 and S10. Color of MOFs-2 was changed from blue to dark purple after loading of DOX (Fig. S9). Obviously decrease in absorption spectra of DOX solution was found, accompanied with the disappearance of red1 color of the supernatant. As shown in Fig. S10, characteristic peaks of DOX at $984\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ was observed in DOX@MOFs-2, which can be attributed to the stretching bands of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ groups. The broad peaks at $1622\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1556\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be assigned to the overlap of stretching vibration of quinone and ketone $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ groups, and the bending bands of the $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ groups at $1610\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . As the result, mixed ligand MOFs-2 was explored as the potential carrier to transport DOX for clinical application.
Next, we examined the controlled release of DOX@MOFs-2 in PBS buffer solution with different pH level, and the result was shown in Fig. 6b. In contrast with the slow and partly release of DOX@MOFs-2 in neutral and weak acid solution, completely DOX release in $80\mathrm{h}$ was observed when the DOX@MOFs-2 was incubated in the acidic solution $\mathrm{(pH~}3.8\mathrm{)}$ . We reasoned that this is because the decomposition of MOFs in acid buffer which was induced by the protonation of IPA and BTC [48]. To verify the decomposition of MOFs after drug releasing, ICP-MS was performed to quantify the Cu ions in supernatant of the DOX@MOFs-2. As shown in Table S1 in the revised manuscript, Cu ions was then determined to be $192.4\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ in PBS of $\mathsf{p H}3.8$ while less increase in the concentration of Cu ions was observed at $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ . The results confirmed that the release of DOX due the decomposition of DOX $@$ MOFs-2 in acidic PBS solution. Considering the acidic of tumor cells displayed, functional mixed ligand MOFs2 could be employed as the transport vehicle for effective tumor chemotherapy as the result of high loading capacity and controlled release.
## 4. Conclusions
In the present work, MOFs with different organic ligand, BTC and IPA, were designed and successfully synthesized by robust hydro-thermal method. The structures of four MOFs, MOFs-1, MOFs-2, MOFs-3, and MOFs-4, were confirmed by XRD, FTIR analysis, and their morphologies were visualized by SEM imaging analysis. In contrast to the control groups with single ligand framework, mixed-ligand MOFs, MOFs-2, and MOFs-3 showed negligible cytotoxicity towards human normal cells, HEK 293A, which enable them to be used as the potential porous materials for drug loading and release. The capability of synthesized MOFs as the delivery vehicles were then evaluated by loading and controlled release of IBU and DOX. The results showed that mixed ligand MOFs-2 with $40\%$ BTC, $60\%$ IPA had the best performance on drug loading and releasing. We expect that the success of the present findings will not only provide a new approach for the IBU and DOX delivery, but also pave the way for the development of new porous MOFs for the practical bio-applications.
